no.

---

### Step 15: Explore the zero set of \(h(t)\)

Recall \(h(t) = 0\) only when either \(|t|=0.25\) or \(|t|=0.75\):
- At \(t=\pm 0.25\),
- At \(t=\pm 0.75\).

Using this:

- \(y = 0\) implies
\[
0 = h(\sin(2\pi x)) \implies \sin(2\pi x) = \pm 0.25 \quad \text{or} \quad \pm 0.75.
\]
Similarly,
- \(x = 0\) implies
\[
0 = h(\cos(3 \pi y)) \implies \cos(3\pi y) = \pm 0.25 \quad \text{or} \quad \pm 0.75.
\]

---

### Step 16: Summary so far

The system is:
\[
\begin{cases}
y = h(\sin(2\pi x)), \\
x = h(\cos(3\pi y)).
\end{cases}
\]

Where
\[
h(t) = 
\begin{cases}
4|0.25 - |t||, & |t| \leq 0.5, \\
4||t| - 0.75|, & |t| \geq 0.5,
\end{cases}
\quad t \in [-1,1].
\]

Note that \(h\) is piecewise linear on intervals \([0,0.25]\), \([0.25,0.5]\), \([0.5,0.75]\), and \([0.75,1]\).

